#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082971 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8922ab07-5ac7-5f85-bfee-c30de7f4024a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "15c029df-b002-51cc-87db-a94d40a02771" :question/math-academy-id "e-595" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\cos{t}, \\quad y=\\sin{t}, \\qquad  0\\leq t<2\\pi,\n$$\n\n calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14077511/assets/2ec95dd061fa6b7f09c9f899a1f6483b4ea7d5127372ddd6eefb74d6ed10c14c.png)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -\\sin{t}, \\qquad y'(t)=\\cos{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\cos t}{-\\sin t} = -\\cot t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082972 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bda136-40a9-576a-b85c-4d4ebefaeb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "148e5378-9177-59ba-9b1e-3633c940babd" :question/math-academy-id "e-596" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=te^t, \\quad y=e^{-t}, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = e^t + te^t = (1+t)e^t, \\qquad y'(t)=-e^{-t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{-{e}^{-t}}{(1+t){e}^{t}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(1+t){e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2(1)}(1+1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8922ab07-5ac7-5f85-bfee-c30de7f4024a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7479fc56-b1d6-5832-8780-701555ad4f83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384407} {:answer/id #uuid "3c264130-b076-5126-b15d-095f695b8dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384408} {:answer/id #uuid "cf00900f-0e76-5f82-90fe-345951cc4279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384409} {:answer/id #uuid "88916111-09cc-5353-91e0-95968d526718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384410} {:answer/id #uuid "9b43152e-5dab-5d2e-9162-53a203de03d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b43152e-5dab-5d2e-9162-53a203de03d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384411} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c9027d3-9084-52ea-a1c8-49b25edddbc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c14e172-0d42-58ef-86b2-05fb93de9caa" :question/math-academy-id "q-20990" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14077511/assets/5855e92b04ffbf8447d32c7edb190cd16046a13e8808e6ffaade1f59e414c869.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=2t{e}^{t},\\,y={t}^{2}+2t,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(t+1),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t+2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2t+2}{2{e}^{t}(t+1)}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bda136-40a9-576a-b85c-4d4ebefaeb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "952e3d08-f150-517b-a16b-e760cbc4bca2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384414} {:answer/id #uuid "138c4bfd-92b3-5bcb-b773-aee7364dc940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "952e3d08-f150-517b-a16b-e760cbc4bca2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384414} :answer-field/id #uuid "73fff2be-be7b-5566-b79e-3d5ec3045bcb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ae411a5-1447-590c-b31c-0b3da5778742" :question/math-academy-id "q-348304" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14077511/assets/7ae65c873838ede4cce89c953ff2654fa1e58e65fdc87896c8ba85bbf947be20.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /4} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384416 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8922ab07-5ac7-5f85-bfee-c30de7f4024a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fe12c1a-816d-5bf3-b6e6-5e2c36ca5044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fe12c1a-816d-5bf3-b6e6-5e2c36ca5044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384418} :answer-field/id #uuid "31ea4f65-6086-59c4-80ad-2f87661f2742" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a75626e-e33d-522c-9cd4-0c52b5a43afb" :question/math-academy-id "q-353346" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{t},\\,y=8t-3,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=4.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,4}=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{8}{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}})}=16\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=4$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,4} & =16\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =16(2) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bda136-40a9-576a-b85c-4d4ebefaeb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "181c81c7-97db-5341-a50d-fc0697d10adf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "181c81c7-97db-5341-a50d-fc0697d10adf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384421} :answer-field/id #uuid "df771847-66bc-58d7-a898-aae8c817eab2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9226c1a9-368c-5df7-b150-42ad7ca22abf" :question/math-academy-id "q-353350" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14077511/assets/abab926e465522d30b236577bf8a1654502807b864c9fed8a4cebb84efffd3a2.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /4} & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bda136-40a9-576a-b85c-4d4ebefaeb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7dfe366-6105-5e10-aefd-217ec1baad4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7dfe366-6105-5e10-aefd-217ec1baad4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384424} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d89fe0c-fcc2-58fa-a0d7-3c915d13cefd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4242fbb0-335c-5f3d-98d5-a78820c5ed85" :question/math-academy-id "q-353351" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14077511/assets/35c62ccf48c4d0a49729da04b443cb94d112e673e0c24e7784d22f48eaabf8f7.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /3} & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bda136-40a9-576a-b85c-4d4ebefaeb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ac5c850-68c2-59e8-a44d-fefa180efc41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15e^3\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ac5c850-68c2-59e8-a44d-fefa180efc41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15e^3\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384427} :answer-field/id #uuid "2be19854-b6fc-5df7-9700-ba7007a4872f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cb37c8f1-fe89-5b06-b034-120e935850f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-363490" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{3/t},\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}},\\,0<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{3/t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}})}{(-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{3/t})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3/t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3({1}^{2}+4)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3(5)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15\\sqrt{5}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{75{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bda136-40a9-576a-b85c-4d4ebefaeb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a138017d-47fa-598d-a933-92367eee3825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082317} {:answer/id #uuid "b6dbfd57-bd52-5653-9348-bec77c8949d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082318} {:answer/id #uuid "e3adecf5-9d81-5833-a254-fb447b141534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082319} {:answer/id #uuid "3534a217-beb4-51f9-b215-8e2b858e9868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082320} {:answer/id #uuid "3a833825-f97a-5e8b-8374-759b0958c9c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3534a217-beb4-51f9-b215-8e2b858e9868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082320} :answer-field/id #uuid "7866304e-1f46-5cfd-ba9b-9131acf20f8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a73a45f9-70e6-5c98-9255-49149400966d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6808" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14077511/assets/1739317bf1b1c567f74dccf5b24a1b1dde8eb101d20ceec9652f604dc6f84543.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /6} & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384428 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8922ab07-5ac7-5f85-bfee-c30de7f4024a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35b96926-dfc1-5943-8eeb-63b2188a7e89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384430} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd8cdd1-ccb2-57a1-8a59-12bb80685985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384431} {:answer/id #uuid "3629b737-9a93-5f22-ae3f-bb5f72ec5d2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384432} {:answer/id #uuid "f75445e9-a538-5d3a-8784-9907ee2500b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384433} {:answer/id #uuid "e33560ef-2158-59fd-a1a2-3ee407e31f86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f75445e9-a538-5d3a-8784-9907ee2500b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384433} :answer-field/id #uuid "f342eb28-10e1-5c20-b0a9-4c27073ec6aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "10c1d858-e83c-53dc-8a2e-e0da605f9c99" :question/math-academy-id "q-6810" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14077511/assets/4969bf3cea8290c76e79419ed0160a8c1f92d7415250305df56eb8128ad15cf4.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}+t-1,\\,y={t}^{3},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}+1,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{4{t}^{3}+1}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}}{4(-1)^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(-4)+1} \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8922ab07-5ac7-5f85-bfee-c30de7f4024a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6af43bb8-304b-555c-8837-3a555d3102f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384437} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f2e2fa-6c8b-54c1-8637-42aaea152b46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{2e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384438} {:answer/id #uuid "d40e9b67-9257-5078-ab06-3a3c549e5c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384439} {:answer/id #uuid "5bd0872e-e79a-54be-b6b9-64551ef91d8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384440} {:answer/id #uuid "fb48ffe7-49a9-57a8-811b-31c6ac3a78f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{3e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bd0872e-e79a-54be-b6b9-64551ef91d8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384440} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e1bc926-23da-52c0-ab31-2a7bc185cbe5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef78af33-68fa-5c96-9f7c-05772b13cffb" :question/math-academy-id "q-6812" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=({t}^{3}-1){e}^{t},\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=({t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-1){e}^{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{({t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-1){e}^{t}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{({0}^{3}+3\\cdot {0}^{2}-1){e}^{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)}{(-1)\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8922ab07-5ac7-5f85-bfee-c30de7f4024a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "579eb472-571a-5c76-ba3e-bfab0f3563ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384444} {:answer/id #uuid "982fad9e-2c8e-50a1-84b9-2cd96d779391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384445} {:answer/id #uuid "3dc3c435-e244-5fa3-8b37-8acebd6a535f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384446} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0c5fef-ac3f-5660-b978-4c0ea4badf1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384447} {:answer/id #uuid "ea0cd850-f31e-5e1f-b26f-b0e2cd5fa37b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea0cd850-f31e-5e1f-b26f-b0e2cd5fa37b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384448} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fd3893f-7eb0-5ae8-a9c8-9e7b9e5d4e75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7552a8e3-52dc-5d1a-9c2d-0034755ae63f" :question/math-academy-id "q-6848" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14077511/assets/5d4abbff66a225f92a8e4f5d30fb86b0b8e205b8a047ed3ed9d5af7290ac89c6.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={t}^{5}-2t,\\,y=1-{t}^{3},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=5{t}^{4}-2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-3{t}^{2}}{5{t}^{4}-2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{-3(-1)^{2}}{5(-1)^{4}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{5-2} \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
